{
  "id": "Compound::DB01156 - Gene::1559",
  "verbalization": "Bupropion is implicated in the activation of Cyp2c9 through a series of interconnected pathways that involve both direct and indirect mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Bupropion may influence Cyp2c9 via the production of stress symptoms, hyperchlorhydria, and epicondylitis, all of which are mediated by other agents that bind to Cyp2c9. These agents, such as Zolpidem, Alosetron, and Celecoxib, are linked to Bupropion through a common pathway involving the nervous system and organic compounds. The shared role of Bupropion in initiating these effects, coupled with the binding of various agents to Cyp2c9, indicates a potential mechanism where Bupropion indirectly affects Cyp2c9 through the production of metabolites or by influencing the activity of agents that bind to the enzyme. This suggests a broader interaction between Bupropion and Cyp2c9, possibly through the modulation of metabolic pathways or the activation of enzymes in the nervous system.",
